# Benchmark summary

## ❌ 4 gates failed

Three things can fail a benchmark run, because only they are properties of a single run or ratios measured within it, or a decision a human must make, so they depend far less on the machine than a raw time does: a function whose measured growth class differs from its documented big-O, the package's overhead relative to its bare baseline growing past a threshold against the last run on main and the last release, and a function whose growth class got worse than at the last release without a recorded exception. A function whose big-O could not be determined is a warning, because nothing is known to be wrong. A raw time is never compared between runs, because runs on different machines are not comparable.

- ❌ **Runtime** — `fn:alpha`: documented O(n), measured O(n²) (exponent 2.04, R² 0.99). Either the code regressed or the documented big-O is wrong -- decide which and fix it.
- ❌ **Runtime** — `fn:beta`: documented constant, measured linear (exponent n/a, R² n/a). Either the code regressed or the documented big-O is wrong -- decide which and fix it.
- ❌ **Runtime** — Overhead relative to the baseline grew from 100.0% (last run on main) to 400.0% -- up 300%, over the 50% allowed for work this fast. Both figures are same-run ratios, so this is not a slower machine.
- ❌ **Runtime** — Overhead relative to the baseline grew from 100.0% (last release) to 400.0% -- up 300%, over the 50% allowed for work this fast. Both figures are same-run ratios, so this is not a slower machine.
- ℹ️ **Runtime** — The previous run used Node v20.0.0 and this one Node v22.1.0: raw time deltas are not comparable (the engine differs), only the same-run gates are meaningful.
- ℹ️ **Runtime** — The previous run was on CPU B and this one on CPU A: raw time deltas compare two different machines.

